      "math_academy_id": "e-13958",
      "problem": "Given the inequalities $x < 1,$  $x \\geq  0,$ and $-2 < x < 4,$ express their common set of solutions as an interval.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/2544eed03d4349fdd897e62447a9566843c53c7ab24001525d5148dba14fecd4.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}\\left[0, 1\\right)}.$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is included in the intersection since it lies in all three intervals. On the other hand, the point $1$ is not included in the intersection because it does not lie in the first interval.",
